WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
 In 2010, as part of their
tenth-anniversary celebrations,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.120
Google awarded huge sums of money
to five projects,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.600
small companies, startups and inventions
that they thought could change the world.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.120
Those five included some names
that you might recognise:

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.560
the most famous is Khan Academy,

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.300
which was only a
couple of years old then.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.180
But Google also gave
one million dollars

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.360
to an invention intended to
revolutionise public transit:

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.680
the Shweeb.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.020
This was the prototype,
set up as a racetrack,

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.700
and it's still here
in New Zealand.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.420
- Shweeb is effectively
a bicycle on a monorail.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.300
These have been specifically
designed so that

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.000
they can swing horizontally,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.840
we get up to speeds of about
60 kilometers an hour,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.460
for someone that's
doing really well.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.020
The original theme of this was to
turn them into a transport system.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.080
The racetrack, in our environment,
is certainly better

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.340
because we're an adventure park.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.060
If it was just a setup where we
would just cruise around the park,

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.460
we don't think it would've
had enough impact.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.500
The electric sides of them
are off-the-shelf.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.620
Everything else is built
by us here in Rotorua.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.220
The initial ones were built with
Geoff Barnett, the inventor,

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.080
when he first got the ride going.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.840
They then adapted that
very quickly,
 &nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.940
because there was a few
things that didn't work.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.220
And then when there was
a change in management,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.260
we made electric pedal assists.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.660
There was the family market
that were dying to do it,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.700
but they found it a bit too hard
to get them going.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.180
2017-18 is when the
next generation,

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.040
the third generation
of pods came in.

00:01:26.135 --> 00:01:27.135
[screaming]

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.640
Sorry about that.

00:01:30.383 --> 00:01:32.589
- There are some obvious
drawbacks with the design.

00:01:32.589 --> 00:01:34.635
What if the person in front
pedals slowly,

00:01:34.635 --> 00:01:36.780
how do you overtake?
You can't.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.120
The marketing copy for the
Shweeb says that instead,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.700
you just make contact
with the car in front

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:42.780
and push it along too,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.400
and build up a train
of these pods

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.500
moving along the track,
all working together.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.260
How about uphills?
What if you get tired?

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.760
Or downhills, if you
need to brake?

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.860
Or what if you're approaching
a track switch

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.840
and it's not locked
into place yet?

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.180
Well, the marketing says that
in the final version,

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.880
there'd be electric boosters
available on uphills,

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.560
which, to be fair, there are
now in this version,

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.360
and also brakes on each pod,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.620
and a control centre somewhere
to watch over traffic and safety.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.580
Which adds such a ridiculous amount
of complexity and points of failure.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.240
And their answer for "what if you're
wearing a skirt in this position" was:

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.196
"don't".

00:02:16.196 --> 00:02:17.700
- It has to swing.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.240
You would have too much tension
and torsion on the pods

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.640
if it stayed straight
as you were going this way.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.680
So it is designed to swing,
and it's got dampeners

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.300
so that it takes up the
swinging motion and slows it down.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.080
It's one of the rides here at
Velocity Valley that anybody can do,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.600
from three years old,
right up to 90 years old.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.020
- This is what transit nerds
call a "gadgetbahn":

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.360
something that looks and
sounds futuristic,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.120
but is actually worse than a
more down-to-earth option.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.420
Instead of this, cities could
just build separated bike lanes,

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.600
they'd handle more people,
reach further, cost far less,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.660
require very little maintenance,
and allow overtaking.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.060
It's pretty obvious that the Shweeb

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.640
was not the worldwide
future of public transit,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:04.980
but somehow, out of 150,000 ideas,
Google looked at this and said,

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.680
yeah, spend a million dollars
on more studies

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.060
to see if it can get rolled out.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.340
And the prototype was already
built at the time!

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.820
A lot of the media back then
were... skeptical.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:16.800
- They had lots of opportunities.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.200
Most people know about
the Google campus.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.840
They also had the Olympics,
they had BP oil.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.340
They had these big ones lined up,

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.200
but they just never came through.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.340
And so we actually bought the IP and
everything off the original owners.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.100
We've already got a franchise
started in Korea,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.920
so we're gonna be working pretty hard
to get more on the go.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.060
More as a ride experience.
We're still in that zone where

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.080
to turn it into a transport system
takes a lot of work.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.620
But yeah, it's definitely something
that everybody asks us about.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.540
- I will say, though: this is fun.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.400
There is a sort of
retro-futuristic kick

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.560
in pedalling your own
monorail pod.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.020
I did try to reach out to the
original inventor for comment,

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.180
because of course, that's good practice
for something like this,

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.580
but I coudn't even find
a way to contact him.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.220
Please don't try,
if you're watching this?

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.620
Just... please don't. I'd guess
he wants to be left in peace,

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.060
hopefully you can
respect that as well.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.020
Truth is, I don't know how I'd feel
if something I'd invented,

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.120
something that got a
million dollars in funding,

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.580
something that presumably
I'd had faith in

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:15.780
and worked on for years...

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.960
ended up being a strange
novelty ride for tourists.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.780
But I do know
I'd be really annoyed

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:23.940
if people kept asking me about it.

